<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert Speer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert Speer was a German architect and Nazi politician who became Hitler's favourite architect and, from 1942, Minister of Armaments and War Production. By rationalising industry and using forced labour he raised arms output to a peak in 1944. Tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, he admitted general responsibility while denying knowledge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, served twenty years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spandau">Spandau</a> prison and wrote best-selling memoirs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-speer.jpg" alt="Albert Speer" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Binder (maybe Atelier Binder after Alexander Binders death in 1929) · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146II-277,_Albert_Speer_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
